      "question": {
        ":question/id": "386b29aa-4458-560c-9279-5b720c1afe27",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)= (y + 1)\\ln (x + 4),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}((y + 1)\\ln (x + 4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y + 1}{x + 4} \\\\ \\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}((y + 1)\\ln (x + 4)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x + 4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y + 1}{x + 4} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 4) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y + 1}{x + 4} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 4) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y + 1}{x + 4}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x + 4)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y + 1=0,$ which means $y=-1.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x + 4)=0,$ which means that $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(-3,-1).$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
