      "question": {
        ":question/id": "d1ec7812-3978-5a02-ac42-759b550f6d05",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)= (y - 1)\\ln (x + 2),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}((y - 1)\\ln (x + 2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y - 1}{x + 2} \\\\ \\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}((y - 1)\\ln (x + 2)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x + 2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y - 1}{x + 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y - 1}{x + 2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y - 1}{x + 2}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x + 2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y - 1=0,$ which means $y=1.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x + 2)=0,$ which means that $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(-1,1).$",
